f 17592186045417] [17592186049801 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "14fbe17c-9f56-5dba-963a-a64e379ba05f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1031 "Properties of Expectation for Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1033 2836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050775 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "65bfe855-0363-58b6-a91d-f3f12be3a932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186153873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153868 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e56bd0b8-dd44-5b86-a00c-3b8ef788a24e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153869 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9c823865-e861-5d67-a8dd-12a609a5d5ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153870 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{75}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153870 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153870 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c54e1733-eedb-5175-93f7-00f0ff85140d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153871 #edb/ref 1080 "11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153871 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153871 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12aa8baa-a74b-5d99-92f6-b0ee93089a8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153872 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{35}{24}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153872 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a8339af-aa00-5d70-912d-8abbe719a9a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153873 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{8}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153873 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153873 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "60e2dd92-ba17-5b89-b3ee-88e17ff1d026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156994 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156994 #edb/ref 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n$f(x) = 2x, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1$\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}$, compute $\\textrm{E}[5X^2 - 2X]$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\textrm{E}[X] + b\\textrm{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65957407-b302-51c5-93d9-0f71f3517362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156994 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X^{2} - 2X] &= 5E[X^{2}] - 2E[X]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\textrm E [X]$. So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot 2xdx \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}x^{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X^{2} - 2X] &= 5E[X^{2}] - 2E[X] \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156995 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156995 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{8},\\begin{vmatrix}-2 < x < 0, \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x < 1$.\n\nCalculate $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "292610ec-9c27-590d-b207-c76adc959d55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156995 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the expected value of $X^2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_{S} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{-2}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{0}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}dx + \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\\int_{-2}^{0}x^{2}dx + \\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{24}x^{3}\\right|_{-2}^{0} + \\left.\\frac{3}{8}x^{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{24}(0 + 8) + \\frac{3}{8}(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{3}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{24}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156996 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156996 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${1 - x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}1 < x \\le 2$.\n\nCalculate $\\textrm E [X^4]$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c5666a4-f477-5b1a-8d17-1e2aa594863f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156996 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$, the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_{S} x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{4}] &= \\int_{0}^{2}x^{4} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{4} \\cdot (1 - x)dx + \\int_{1}^{2}x^{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{4} - x^{5})dx + \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{2}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(\\frac{1}{5}x^{5} - \\frac{1}{6}x^{6})\\right|_{0}^{1} + \\left.\\frac{1}{10}x^{5}\\right|_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{6} - 0) + \\frac{1}{10}(32 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{30} + \\frac{31}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{47}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157393 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157393 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157393 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157393 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157393 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186156994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157393 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157394 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157394 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157394 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157394 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157394 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157394 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186156995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157394 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157395 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157395 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157395 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157395 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186156996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157395 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1045 "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "55c2127a-d43f-53c5-907e-c8d12316517c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157604 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157604 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{3}{x^{4}},\\begin{vmatrix}1 < x \\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3x^{3}},\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le x < 4$.\n\nCalculate $E[X^{4}]$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50e246df-b4af-588d-81cb-30d14deb51ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1061 39753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dc1ae12-0989-5596-8114-7d74427919d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158291 #edb/ref 1061 39754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "897313ee-485e-51e5-8fec-b665d482b874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1061 39755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08325c1e-fe0c-5a7b-b6e5-8f27c2951461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1061 39756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bce87273-1a01-58a6-825f-736ef62ef788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1061 39757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fb3611b-a9ba-58f4-9159-00a7c9b29393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$.\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X$, respectively.\n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$, the expected value of the the random variable $X^2$, we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{S}x^{2} \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{5/2}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}x^{7/2})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1065 6591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1064 "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X$.\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\displaystyle \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFinally, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1065 6592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1067 "Higher Moments" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
